Table 7. Overview result of models
Based on the results obtained from the two datasets, Celeb-DF and FaceForensics++, a significant difference in performance between the model architectures and the extent to which data features influence deepfake detection capabilities can be observed. This comparison clarifies the advantages and disadvantages of each model and evaluates its generalizability under different data conditions.
Considering the Celeb-DF dataset, the BiGRU model combining Multi-Head Self-Attention and KAN showed the best and most stable performance. This model achieved an average accuracy of 0.8348 along with an AUC value of up to 0.9186, reflecting its ability to effectively distinguish between real and fake video. Precision, Recall, and F1-score metrics were all balanced, indicating that the model not only accurately predicted but also maintained good deepfake detection capabilities without sacrificing a high false alarm rate. The moderate variation between folds suggests the model's ability to learn general features on a highly complex dataset like Celeb-DF.
Compared to BiGRU, the BiLSTM model combining Attention and KAN on Celeb-DF achieved slightly lower average accuracy. Although Precision reached a fairly high value, Recall decreased significantly, resulting in a lower F1-score. This suggests the model tends towards cautious prediction, prioritizing the reduction of false positives but not being truly strong in detecting all deepfake patterns. Nevertheless, the stability between the folds of this model is relatively good, reflecting stable convergence during training.
Conversely, the TCN model with residual blocks showed significantly poor performance on the Celeb-DF dataset. The average accuracy was only 0.6118, while the F1-score and AUC were also low and varied greatly between folds. This result indicates that the TCN model struggles to handle sophisticated and diverse deepfake operations, which are a prominent characteristic of Celeb-DF. The TCN's strong reliance on long-term context may not be effective in cases where the forgery signatures appear intermittently or are dispersed over time.
On the FaceForensics++ dataset, the performance trends among the models show a notable shift. The BiGRU model, combining Multi-Head Self-Attention and KAN, continues to demonstrate high and stable performance, with an average accuracy of 0.8490 and an AUC of 0.9369. Notably, the high Recall value indicates the model's ability to detect deepfakes effectively on this dataset. This suggests that the architecture combining a two-dimensional sequential model and a multi-head attention mechanism effectively exploits the consistent spatio-temporal features in FaceForensics++.
The BiLSTM model combining Attention and KAN in FaceForensics++ achieved relatively high average accuracy; however, results varied significantly between folds. Some folds performed very well, even approaching perfection, while one fold yielded unusually low results. This reflects the model's sensitivity to data partitioning and also indicates that the generalizability of BiLSTM–Attention–KAN is not yet stable when data distribution changes.
Notably, the TCN model with residual blocks performed very well on FaceForensics++. This model achieved an average accuracy of 0.8410, an F1-score of 0.8542, and an AUC of 0.9348, along with low variability between folds. This result suggests that TCN is capable of effectively exploiting long-term features and repetitive spoofing structures, which are common in FaceForensics++. This explains why TCN performed better on FaceForensics++ but less so on Celeb-DF.
Synthesizing the results from the two datasets shows that the BiGRU model combining Multi-Head Self-Attention and KAN is the architecture with the best performance and generalization capabilities. This model maintained high performance on both datasets, demonstrating flexibility and adaptability to different deepfake features. Meanwhile, BiLSTM–Attention–KAN is suitable for scenarios with relatively stable structured data, while TCN–Residual shows clear strengths on highly sequential and patterned datasets, but is less effective when faced with complex and diverse deepfake operations.
CHAPTER 5: APPLICATION DESIGN AND IMPLEMENTATION
5.1 Chapter Overview
The design and implementation of a digital banking system integrating deepfake facial fraud detection within the context of eKYC/user authentication. The goal of this chapter is to demonstrate the feasibility of the proposed solution in the previous chapters with a practical implementation architecture: from facial data acquisition, feature extraction, model inference, to decision integration into the banking business flow (authentication – transaction – alert).

Figure 6. Illustrates the “end-to-end” flow of the system
(i) The user performs facial authentication on the mobile application (camera); (ii) the video-streaming module receives and organizes data according to session; (iii) the deepfake-extractor module extracts features from two directions: behavioral/face features and image features, and combines them with the frequency/forensic domain feature group FFT/DCT/SRM in the Fusion block; (iv) the Detection module performs time-series classification using sequential models (BiLSTM/BiGRU/TCN); (v)The results are stored (PostgreSQL) and integrated into core-banking functions such as Authentication, Transaction, and Alert to complete the business response cycle.
In the following sections of the chapter, the thesis presents: (1) the architecture and design of the main modules;(2) the functional and non-functional requirements of the system and (3) the UI/UX design for the application flow.
5.2. System Components
The Deepfake Banking system is designed with a client-server (online) architecture, implemented with clear layering and modular separation to ensure scalability, ease of maintenance, and integration into banking operations. The entire data flow is processed from left to right (according to the architectural diagram), starting from the user's device (camera on the mobile app), passing through the video/frame reception and coordination layer, AI processing for feature extraction and deepfake inference, and then the results are sent to the Core Banking System for business decision-making (allowing/rejecting transactions, issuing alerts).
Essentially, the server-side organizational system is organized into four main functional service blocks operating in a microservices flow: (1) Mobile Application, (2) Video-Streaming Service, (3) Deepfake Detection System, and (4) Core Banking System. Each block performs a separate role, while coordinating closely through APIs and session management mechanisms to ensure data consistency and traceability when audits are needed.
5.2.1 Mobile Application (React Native Expo)
The mobile application serves as the primary user interface and the starting point for the deepfake authentication mechanism. The application was developed using React Native Expo to support multiple platforms (iOS/Android), and TypeScript to improve code quality and reduce runtime errors. Screen navigation is organized using Expo Router.
In the deepfake authentication step, when a user performs a sensitive operation (typically a money transfer), the application prompts the user to authenticate their face via the camera. A short video/snippet is recorded, then converted into a frame sequence and sent to the Video-Streaming Service using the session ID. This design allows the authentication process to occur near real-time without interrupting the user experience, while also reducing the risk of data mix-ups between sessions.
5.2.2. API Gateway
The API Gateway is positioned at the edge layer as a reverse proxy, ensuring all mobile app traffic passes through a single "gateway" before entering core banking, video streaming, and deepfake services. The gateway is responsible for routing/load balancing and applying connection policies at the edge level, while also supporting reverse proxying for socket connections (WebSocket/streaming) to maintain a real-time data channel (frame/chunk pushing and processing status reception) with lower latency than polling.
5.2.2 Video-Streaming Service (Ingestion and forwarding)
The data organization mechanism is based on the key pair `{userId}/{sessionId}`. Each authentication session creates its own temporary storage area to: receive frames in chronological order, temporarily store them as image files (or video chunks), record session metadata (session creation time, upload status, number of frames, device, etc.), and trigger a processing job in the Deepfake Detection System.
In practical implementation, the heavy processing (feature extraction + inference) should be managed asynchronously (job/queue) to avoid timeouts and increase stability under high load. However, at the demo scale, minimal synchronous implementation is possible, provided that session state tracking is still ensured (UPLOADING → UPLOADED → PROCESSING → DONE/FAILED).
5.2.3 Deepfake Detection System (AI Processing Pipeline)
The Deepfake Detection System is the AI ​​processing center of the system. Its main goal is to analyze the frame sequence captured from the camera to detect whether the video shows signs of deepfake. The AI ​​architecture is designed in three main steps: (i) feature extraction, (ii) feature fusion, and (iii) time-series detection. OpenFace extracts features related to faces and behavior such as landmarks, head pose, gaze, action units, blink signals, etc. MobileNetV2 extracts deep learning image features (embedding) from frames, and Feature Fusion combines them with frequency domain forensic features and structural noise: such as FFT (Fast Fourier Transform), DCT (Discrete Cosine Transform), and SRM (Spatial Rich Model) to detect traces of noise/texture and microscopic artifacts often found in fake images.
Detection Module – Time Series Classification
The final step involves performing classification on the time-series feature sequence. The system tests deep learning models suitable for the time series data: BiLSTM, BiGRU, and TCN.
The output of the Detection Module is a predictive label (real/fake or risk level) along with a confidence score. The confidence score is used to make business decisions (allow, request re-verification, or deny/push moderation).
5.2.4 Core Banking System (Golang + Clean Architecture)
The Core Banking System is the central processing unit for banking operations, responsible for functions such as user authentication, account management, transaction processing, and security alert management. The system is implemented using Go (Golang) with a Clean Architecture approach to ensure performance, layer separation, and scalability. The core data is stored in PostgreSQL with full ACID properties to ensure transaction security. In the context of deepfake integration, the core banking system receives results from the Deepfake Detection System and uses those results as a risk control signal.
If the result is safe (real, high confidence), the system allows the transaction to continue. If there is suspicion or suspicion of fraud, the system rejects the transaction, generates a security alert, logs the audit, and sends a notification to the user.
