5.3.2. Non-functional requirements
Besides login/registration, account management, transaction processing, and deepfake detection integration, the UIT Banking system needs to meet non-functional requirements to ensure stable and secure operation in the digital banking environment, especially when handling sensitive data (accounts, transactions, faces).
Performance: The system must respond reliably to frequent operations such as login, account viewing, and transaction history. The deepfake detection process (video/frame capture → feature extraction → inference) requires appropriate processing time to avoid interrupting the transaction creation experience; when processing is lengthy, the system must display status and avoid request hangs.
Availability: Core functions need to operate continuously, minimizing downtime. When the AI ​​module fails or becomes overloaded, the system needs to transition to a safe processing state (retry/postpone/deny depending on policy) instead of causing the entire system to shut down.
Data Reliability & Integrity. Transactions must ensure atomicity and data consistency (avoiding incomplete balance updates). With deepfake detection, data must be correctly assigned `userId/sessionId` to ensure the results return the correct authentication session in progress.
Security. The system needs centralized authentication and access control (token/JWT), and mechanisms to limit access frequency to reduce the risk of abuse/attacks. Sensitive actions such as creating transactions and deepfake authentication must be more tightly protected than normal information queries.
Scalability. Because AI processing is resource-intensive, the system needs to allow the deepfake detection module to be separated into a distinct service and scale according to load without affecting the core banking and mobile app.
Maintainability. The architecture needs to be modular so that the model (BiLSTM/BiGRU/TCN) or features (OpenFace, MobileNetV2, FFT/DCT/SRM) can be changed without disrupting the banking business logic; model configuration and versions must be clearly managed.
Deployment and Portability Services should be easy to package and deploy in server/container environments, ensuring reconfigurability between development and demo/production environments.
