CHAPTER 6: CONCLUSION
6.1 Summary and Key Contributions
This thesis addresses facial fraud in digital banking by moving beyond simple real/fake classification and jointly analyzing both forgery traces and the naturalness of facial behavior over time. The proposed approach integrates two complementary signal groups: frequency-domain forensic features (FFT, DCT, SRM) and behavioral-physiological cues such as blinking, gaze direction, and head movement, which are difficult to consistently reproduce in deepfakes.
To model temporal dynamics, three sequence-processing architectures were implemented and compared: BiGRU with Multi-Head Self-Attention and KAN, BiLSTM with Attention and KAN, and TCN with Residual Blocks. In addition to algorithmic research, the thesis demonstrates a research-to-product workflow by integrating the detection model into a simulated banking system, where authentication results are directly used as risk control signals in transaction processing.
6.2 Achievements and Practical Significance
Experiments on Celeb-DF and FaceForensics++ show clear performance differences among the models. On Celeb-DF, BiGRU + MHA + KAN achieved the best performance (Accuracy 0.8348, AUC 0.9186), followed by BiLSTM + Attention + KAN, while TCN-Residual performed significantly weaker. On FaceForensics++, BiGRU + MHA + KAN remained strong (Accuracy 0.8490, AUC 0.9369), TCN-Residual showed improved stability, and BiLSTM exhibited greater sensitivity to data splits.
Practically, the model is embedded into a simulated banking workflow: legitimate transactions proceed normally, while suspicious sessions trigger transaction blocking, alerts, logging, and user notification. This demonstrates how deepfake detection can function as an operational risk control mechanism rather than an isolated machine learning task.
6.3 Limitations and future development directions
First, the scope of the study is limited to a simulated environment: it does not use real bank data or connect to actual core banking infrastructure; therefore, many real-world implementation factors (regulations, operations, supervision, high load) have not been fully verified.
Next, the stability between folds shows that some architectures are still sensitive to data distribution; for example, BiLSTM can fluctuate strongly from fold to fold, reflecting a generalization capability that is not yet truly "smooth".
Ultimately, at the system layer, there is still room for optimization towards real-time and high-load performance: the heavy processing (feature extraction + inference) is, in the long run, better suited to asynchronous scheduling (job/queue) to avoid timeouts and increase stability.
From the points above, possible development directions include: (i) standardizing session-based streaming mechanisms and optimizing the inference pipeline based on time windows; (ii) adding strategies to reduce domain shift (fine-tune based on target data domains, update controlled model versions); (iii) increasing explanatory power (clarifying time segments/frames that strongly contribute to decision-making); and (iv) perfecting storage and security policies for biometric data, aligning with audit requirements and privacy in the banking environment.
